id: spectator
name: Spectator
kind: state
description: >
  You step out of the fight to watch it, and to steady an ally from the side.
text: >
  (1) [Triggered/Spectator, Triggered/Start of Turn]: Target an Ally. Increase
  their Combat Rolls by 1(bT) until the start of your next turn or until you
  leave the Spectator State (whichever occurs first).

  (2) [Triggered/Start of Turn]: Exit the Spectator State.

  (3) [Automatic/Start of Turn]: If you did not use the second effect of the
  Spectator State, you lose all of your Actions (except Counter Actions), end
  your turn, and regain 2d10(bT) Life and Ki Points.

  (4) [Passive]: You cannot use the Power Up Maneuver, the Transformation
  Maneuver, the Energy Charge Maneuver, or any Attacking Maneuvers, Special
  Maneuvers, or Unique Abilities that target another Character.

  (5) [Passive]: You cannot trigger any [Triggered/Start of Turn] or
  [Triggered/Start of Combat Round] effects, except those from the Spectator
  State.

---

# What is here and what is not.
#
# (2) and (4) are here and are the whole of themselves. (1), (3) and (5) each
# need something this system does not have, and a half-built one would be worse
# than none - each would look like it worked.
#
#   - (1) hands a bonus to somebody else, on a clock, with two ends. Effects reach
#     only the character carrying them: there is no way for a file to say "give
#     that Ally this". Nor to end a duration on the earlier of two conditions.
#   - (3) fires only "if you did not use the second effect", and using (2) leaves
#     the State - so the condition is really "are you still a Spectator when this
#     resolves". But an automatic effect and a triggered one both answer the start
#     of the turn, and the automatic one fires while the player is still being
#     offered the triggered one. Built as it stands, (3) would fire every time and
#     (2) would never get the chance to stop it.
#   - (5) forbids a class of triggers rather than a thing. Nothing can say that.

# (2) is a Maneuver of its own to offer - a triggered effect the player takes at
# the start of their turn - and leaving is all it does.
[triggered/start-of-turn]
leaveState(spectator);

# (4). The three named Maneuvers are named, because the rule names them rather
# than describing them - and two of them do not exist yet, which costs nothing:
# a forbid on a Maneuver nobody holds forbids nothing.
#
# "Special Maneuvers" is not among these. There is no such category in this system
# yet, and inventing one to have something to forbid would be inventing a rule.
[passive]
forbid maneuver.power-up;
forbid maneuver.transformation;
forbid maneuver.energy-charge;
forbid attackingManeuvers;
forbid uniqueAbilities;
